                <pb facs="1266" n="1116" sameAs="frus:frus1907p2/pg1116"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Wilson to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1907-03-29"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">March 29,
                    1907</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Wilson informs department that he has just received from foreign office the
                    following note verbale: The Government of the King has made known to St.
                    Petersburg that it has no objection that <pb facs="1267" n="1117"
                        xml:id="pg1117"/> the mode of adhesion proposed by the Russian Government be
                    adopted, since this procedure would appear to meet with the assent of all the
                    contracting States required by article 60 of the convention. In using the
                    wording “signatory powers” in the unofficial note of March 23, it was meant to
                    describe by that the “contracting “powers to the terms of the said article 60.
                    Regarding the special situation of Turkey, which signed and did not ratify the
                    convention and to which Russia addressed the circular April 19, 1906, it is
                    supposed that explanations in this respect will ultimately be given. The
                    Imperial Government is alone in a position to reply at the present time to the
                    question. Mr. Wilson states that they are not advised in what terms the
                    invitation to take part in new conference has been addressed by it to the
                    Ottoman Government.)</p>
            </div>
